mode: "100644"
text: "# LinkedIn Mechanics\n\nLinkedIn rewards different behavior than any other platform. Get these mechanics right and an\nordinary post travels; ignore them and a great post dies.\n\n> Specs and algorithm behavior shift; treat numbers as approximate and re-verify quarterly. The\n> principles are durable.\n\n## The two signals that matter most\n\n1. **Dwell time** — how long people spend on the post. LinkedIn reads a long read as a good post.\n   So: write to be read all the way through, and format so people *can*.\n2. **Early, meaningful comments** — especially in the first hour or so. Comments (real ones, not\n   \"Nice!\") signal value and expand reach. The author replying to early comments compounds it.\n\nEverything below serves these two signals.\n\n## The \"…see more\" hook\n\nOn mobile, ~140 characters show before \"…see more\" (a bit more on desktop). That expand tap is the\n**first dwell signal** — if the opening line doesn't earn it, nothing else is seen. Front-load the\nhook (via `hook-writer`); never spend the first line on a greeting, \"I'm excited to announce,\" or\ncontext the reader doesn't have yet.\n\n## Format for dwell (readability, not gimmickry)\n\nLong posts work *only* if they're readable on a phone:\n\n- Short sentences; **one-to-two-line paragraphs**; generous whitespace.\n- A clear build — hook → story/argument → payoff → question.\n- Scannable: a reader skimming should still get the through-line.\n\nThis is whitespace as genuine readability. It is **not** \"broetry\" — the gimmick of one dramatic\nfragment per line for fake profundity (see `avoiding-cringe.md`). Use whitespace to help people\nread, not to manufacture drama.\n\n## Personal vs company\n\nPosts from a **personal profile** (founder, employee) consistently out-reach and out-trust company\npages. People follow people. So:\n\n- **Thought leadership, stories, POV → post from a person** (founder/employee). This is the\n  reach-and-trust play.\n- **Announcements, brand, hiring → the company page is fine**, but pair it with a personal post for\n  reach (employee advocacy).\n\n(This skill writes the post. The account-level system — cadence, commenting, golden hour — is\n`linkedin-growth`; the page strategy and advocacy program are `linkedin-company-pages`.)\n\n## The link problem\n\nLinkedIn suppresses reach on posts with **external links in the body** (it wants people to stay\non-platform). Tactics:\n\n- Put the link in the **first comment** and write \"link in comments.\"\n- Or post **link-free** and let people ask / DM.\n- **Native content beats link posts** — text, document carousels, native video, polls.\n\n## Native formats (use them)\n\n- **Text post** — the default; story/POV/insight.\n- **Document / PDF carousel** — a swipeable PDF; very high dwell + saves. A LinkedIn power format.\n  (Write the post copy here; hand the slides to `carousel-writer`.)\n- **Native video** — uploaded directly (not a YouTube link); captioned for sound-off.\n- **Poll** — strong for quick engagement and a low-effort post.\n- **Newsletter** — for consistent long-form.\n\n## Mechanics checklist\n\n- **Hashtags:** 3–5 relevant, at the end. Not the reach lever they once were.\n- **Length:** up to ~3,000 chars; medium-to-long performs if it earns the read.\n- **Mentions/tags:** tag people only when genuinely relevant (mass-tagging is spammy).\n- **Timing:** professional audience skews weekday business hours (mid-morning often cited) — treat\n  as *general guidance, not the user's own best time* (WoopSocial post analytics are lifetime counts, not a timing model); don't present it as the\n  user's own data.\n- **Author reply move:** reply to early comments (and ask a question) to boost the post — schedule\n  time to be present for the first hour.\n"
